WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:15.880
- [Narrator] Stop-motion animated movies

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.170
have been around a long time,

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.700
but there's a Hollywood
studio solely dedicated

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.950
to bringing the animation
style into the 21st century.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:27.220
Laika Studios is responsible
for movies like "Coraline,"

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.410
"ParaNorman," "Kubo and the Two Strings,"

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.771
and most recently "Missing Link."

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.000
Creating each movie is more painstaking

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.730
than the last for the
Oscar-nominated studio,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.663
with "Missing Link" being
its most ambitious to date.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.620
- I think for me
stop-motion is truly special

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.510
because it has a unique
quality to it that almost go...

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.520
It speaks to your childhood,
when you're a kid,

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.540
and you're playing with toys,

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.350
and you're imagining them coming to life.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.740
- [Narrator] This is Chris Butler.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.010
He's the movie's writer and director.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.930
He decided to direct "Missing Link"

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.160
in the vein of "Raiders of the Lost Ark"

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.370
and "Around the World in 80 Days."

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.323
And it was an idea 15 years in the making.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.150
But first, a little background.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.040
The first stop-motion animation film

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.890
is thought to be 1898's
"The Humpty Dumpty Circus."

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.410
It's a painstaking
process in which objects

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.760
are moved in small
increments, frame by frame.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.260
And though it's difficult,
it's stood the test of time,

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.490
and few places are doing it
better right now than Laika,

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.570
which was first founded in 2005

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.810
and continues to push the envelope

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.440
while remaining true to stop-motion roots.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.320
Take for example its
use of rigging systems

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.360
as seen on this horse puppet.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.510
The rig doesn't help move the puppet

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.550
but rather enables
animators to manipulate it.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.190
This is helpful for puppets
of unusual shapes and sizes

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.980
and thus allows the
animators to dream big.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.280
It also came in handy for some
of the movie's action scenes

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.410
like one that takes place
on a collapsing ice bridge.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.490
- If you think about a character jumping,

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.290
while its feet are on the ground,

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.750
you can manipulate that puppet.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.470
But what do you do for the frames

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:24.700
when its feet are off the ground?

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.500
It can't mysteriously float,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.310
so you have a rig attached to it

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.020
that will support its weight.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.690
- [Narrator] Even with the support system,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.610
this sequence was so complicated

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.210
that it took about a year to shoot.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.967
- Even when I was writing that I thought,

00:02:37.967 --> 00:02:39.630
"I shouldn't be writing this."

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.730
- [Narrator] Butler wanted the
action to feel like something

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.280
you might see in a live-action movie.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.980
In "Missing Link," Hugh
Jackman plays Sir Lionel Frost,

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.400
an adventurer who
discovers the missing link,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.970
known to some as Bigfoot or Sasquatch,

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.570
voiced by Zach Galifianakis.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:57.310
- Well, that didn't go as planned.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.640
- [Narrator] Frost is
given an unlikely task:

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.500
helping unite Link with
his long-lost ancestors.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.850
"Missing Link" is the
first Laika production

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.460
in which all the main
protagonists are adults,

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.290
and it greatly shaped how
the movie was designed.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.330
- You base the scale of the movie

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.870
around the size of your main character.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.470
- [Narrator] So it's not
the puppets themselves

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:18.640
that need to be bigger,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.030
but what they interact
with needs to be smaller.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.900
- There is an optimal size for a puppet.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.810
It's around about 12, 13 inches.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.053
That's a good size.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.270
If you get bigger than that,

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.270
then the poor animator
has to wrestle with it.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.300
If you get smaller than that,

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.730
then you can't get the amount of mechanics

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.803
into the actual puppet that you require.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.800
So that's a good size.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.820
- [Narrator] Sir Lionel
Frost hit the sweet spot

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.340
at about 13 inches tall.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.760
Link, meanwhile, stood
at about 16 inches tall.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.540
The sets on "Missing
Link," Butler estimated,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.410
were about two-thirds the
size of those on "Kubo,"

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.500
which meant they could fit
more sets into one building,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.390
which is very useful for a movie

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.700
that had such a wide array of locations,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.110
including forests, mountains,
and the open ocean.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.160
But you wouldn't call any
of these constructions

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.030
tiny at all.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.210
- I think the hardest
character on "Missing Link"

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.540
was probably Link himself.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.010
He's certainly the most complicated puppet

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.890
that we've ever had at the studio.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.710
I think he took over a year to figure out,

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:22.957
and really it's because he's
covered in hair head to toe.

00:04:22.957 --> 00:04:27.320
And it doesn't help that he
is the shape of an avocado.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.780
He doesn't have a neck.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.390
He's basically a big, cuddly lump.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.640
- [Narrator] Fur might be
the most difficult part

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.660
of the design of a given
claymation character.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.480
Butler and the "Missing Link"
team found a way around this.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.780
They didn't cover Link in actual hair.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:44.860
- I think because of the
stylization of this movie,

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.690
we decided that Link should...

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:47.770
His hair should look the same

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.630
as the hair on the
heads of the characters.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:54.630
So we went for this very
intricate, sculpted tufting,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.610
which was hand-sculpted
in clay and then cast,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.640
and eventually he became
a silicone puppet.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.630
- [Narrator] The world the characters

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.800
are given to inhabit is truly amazing.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.440
The crew built a moving train

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.770
and an accompanying track for it.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.880
A sequence set inside a stagecoach

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.010
was powered by motors and rumble seats.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:17.890
Yet this famously practical
medium isn't CGI-free.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:21.510
The Loch Ness Monster, who
pops up early in the movie,

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.220
required CG animation for
most of its massive body.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.200
The head and neck are practical,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.790
but a lot of what you see underwater

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.070
had to be computer-animated.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.980
This is just one of the
ways in which stop-motion,

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.040
which has been around practically
since the start of movies,

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.620
stays modern without abandoning
what makes it so special.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.150
- I think as long as there
are artists out there

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:48.990
who love and respect the
medium of stop-motion,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.270
there will continue to be stories told.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.170
I don't think it will ever really go away.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.630
It just requires people to champion it.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.080
Certainly at Laika, we love it.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.580
We love the look of it.
We love the feel of it.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.080
We love the artistry of it,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.940
and I think as long as you
can still move with the times,

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.490
you can still use technology to help,

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.970
but you can embrace stop-motion
for what's special about it.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.030
In the end, for me what that means

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.810
is it's real light on real objects,

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:24.370
and there is something about
the imperfection of reality

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:26.573
that is magical.

